identity:
Two layers, files P/sfix/tools/cut.js and ident.js.

(a) Model names (cut.js: masker, modelRanges, learned, candidates, templateOf, codeRanges). A unit's TEMPLATE is its text with every entity form (anywhere) and field name (whole word) replaced by a mark. The SDK name and the language title are always masked. Code spans and code tokens (`data_get()`, `vt->data`, `.ok`, `[]any`) become one placeholder. Articles and numbers are dropped from the key. A candidate is masked only when, among the learners' units of the same template, the name at that place is not always the same. Learners: the OLD pages of all 20 languages, plus the NEW pages of the reference languages (ported.json). The language being masked is left out. If only that language prints the template, its own pages decide. Text that no learner printed as a unit falls back to the v3.2 mask. A name inside code is always masked. TIES: two positions that hold the same name in at least 90% of 3 or more units (planet / PlanetEntity) must still agree, or the later one is marked Ⓔ≠, so an entity swapped in one place is not the template. Composite entity forms (planet_entity) are left out of the learned vocabulary. `learn: false` keeps the exact v3.2 mask for code lines, whole blocks, heading paths, tokens and the ledger's pairing bags, so block class ids and reworded pairing are unchanged.

(b) Sentence identity (ident.js identity). The unit is cut into a skeleton and positions. A position is a code span, a fact phrase, a prose-slot word, or, in a comment, a raw fact token. Candidate values are the union over every provider in the clone. A position is a language slot when its content differs across the reference languages' NEW units with the same skeleton and kind. A slot becomes an unlabelled ⟦⟧; everything else stays literal; numbers become Ⓝ; the article before a mark is normalised. A unit that is all positions (signature heading, key cell, Call cell) keeps its display mask, labels included, so `⟦ref.op.load⟧` and `⟦ref.op.create⟧` stay apart.

cut.js splitSentences now splits after a bold lead ending in . ! or ? (go's `**Create a client.**`); `**Returns:**` is not split.

What no longer splits a sentence (control ids in selftest part identity, all ok):
- printer output `result.ok` / `result["ok"]` / `getp(&result,"ok")`: one id (template-two-langs);
- `direct()` / `Direct()` / `sdk_direct()`: member-name;
- `undefined` / `nil` (noError|none) / `NULL`: label-none;
- `AmbientEntity` / `Ambient` / `Demo.Entity.Ambient` with a/an: article-and-class;
- these 2 / 4 steps: number;
- `Ok`/`Err` vs `.ok`/`.err` in comments: comment-fact;
- list / vector in comments: comment-collection;
- rust `Vec` vs zig slice: vec-own-backticks;
- go bold lead: bold-step-lead;
- backticks inside or outside a mask: ids 092f1dc649 and 2d9199cb7a merged;
- the Control options example: 6 old ids merged into e39f8f7736.

Remaining known splits: a clause that is present in one language and absent in another (`(a dict)` / `(a Hash)` versus no type) is still another sentence; whole-code units differ when their printed tokens differ (`server.tenant` vs `options["server"]["tenant"]`), by design; type cells whose label holds a different set of paths.

masking:
Model names: a word equal to an entity or field name is masked only where, in units of the same template, the word changes from one learner unit to another. The 21 variations name different entities and fields, so a name the model supplied changes and a word of shared prose ("every record is listed", "reads its record", "the SDK utility object", "the entity id") does not. This is sound against a port or a regression because the language under review is never a learner for its own units. A shared word it replaces, even with another entity name, stays a literal and differs from the references.

Facts and printer output: classify.js accepts by the MATCH form (ident.js match). A position is masked only if (1) its raw text is one of the unit language's own declared facts (guarded or accounted), and (2) no other language prints that same text there as plain shared text. Everything else stays literal, so a printer's output must equal a reference's or the class is left for a person, as before. Example of (2): py's `data` is its accessor parameter, but ts, c and the others print `data` plainly in "headers and `data`", so it stays literal. That closes the `data` / `err` / `array` cases where a shared literal was masked because it equalled a fact value.

Controls (controls/masking.json), each a new open class with nothing accepted automatically, in both modes, with v3.3:
- record-to-item-rb, record-to-planet-rb (the brief's control, "without one, every record is listed");
- record-to-utility-go;
- reads-its-utility-ts;
- headers-and-body-py.

The same five under the v3.2 tools (copy in P/sfix/tmp/v32check, log P/sfix/logs/masking-on-v32.log): 3 of 5 not flagged in either mode (record-to-planet-rb, record-to-utility-go, reads-its-utility-ts): 6 of 10 runs failed.

Model texts are left out (ident.js modelTextOf). The strings of every variation's model (entities and the text block, through lib2.makeModel) are normalised (links reduced to their text, `\|` unescaped), and a unit equal to one of them, or a part of one at least 3 words long, is model text. It takes no verdict and is not listed by sentences.js. Both fixture texts are excluded: "Level \| percent ..." and "Words the project chose | not the spec".

